theory.intro:
Every parabola $y=ax^2+bx+c$ has four locating features: the $y$-intercept, the $x$-intercepts, the axis of symmetry and the vertex. This skill finds all four from the equation, without drawing the graph.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\begin{axis}[axis lines=middle, xlabel={$x$}, ylabel={$y$}, xmin=-4.5, xmax=6.8, ymin=-6.3, ymax=4.8, samples=100, clip=false, axis line style={-{Stealth}}, xtick=\empty, ytick=\empty]
\addplot[smooth, thick, blue, <->, domain=-3:5] {0.5*(x+2)*(x-4)};
\addplot[dashed, domain=-6:4] ({1},{x});
\addplot[only marks, mark=*, blue] coordinates {(-2,0) (4,0) (0,-4) (1,-4.5)};
\node[below left] at (axis cs:-2,0) {$\alpha$};
\node[below right] at (axis cs:4,0) {$\beta$};
\node[anchor=east, fill=white, inner sep=1pt] at (axis cs:-0.15,-4) {$c$};
\node at (axis cs:1.85,-5.4) {$V$};
\node[anchor=west] at (axis cs:1.2,4.3) {axis of symmetry};
\end{axis}
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

facts:
A parabola has two, one or no $x$-intercepts, but always exactly one $y$-intercept and one vertex.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\draw[-{Stealth}] (-0.8,0) -- (10.6,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[thick,blue,<->,smooth,variable=\x,domain=0.05:3.15] plot ({\x},{1.2*(\x-1.6)*(\x-1.6)-1.2});
\draw[thick,blue,<->,smooth,variable=\x,domain=4.0:6.4] plot ({\x},{1.2*(\x-5.2)*(\x-5.2)});
\draw[thick,blue,<->,smooth,variable=\x,domain=8.0:10.0] plot ({\x},{1.2*(\x-9.0)*(\x-9.0)+1.2});
\fill (0.6,0) circle (2.5pt);
\fill (2.6,0) circle (2.5pt);
\fill (5.2,0) circle (2.5pt);
\node[below] at (1.6,-1.9) {two $x$-intercepts};
\node[below] at (5.2,-0.7) {one $x$-intercept};
\node[below] at (9.0,-0.7) {no $x$-intercepts};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

foundation.question_text:
A parabola crosses the $x$-axis at $x=-2$ and $x=6$, as shown. Use symmetry to find its axis of symmetry.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\begin{axis}[axis lines=middle, xlabel={$x$}, ylabel={$y$}, xmin=-5, xmax=9, ymin=-8, ymax=6, samples=100, clip=false, axis line style={-{Stealth}}, xtick={-2,2,4,6}, ytick={-6,-3,3}]
\addplot[smooth, thick, blue, <->, domain=-3.57:7.57] {0.4*(x+2)*(x-6)};
\fill (axis cs:-2,0) circle (2.5pt);
\fill (axis cs:6,0) circle (2.5pt);
\node[above left] at (axis cs:-2,0) {$-2$};
\node[above right] at (axis cs:6,0) {$6$};
\end{axis}
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

development.question_text:
A concave-up parabola has an $x$-intercept at $x=-3$, and its axis of symmetry is the line $x=1$. Use symmetry to find the other $x$-intercept.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\begin{axis}[axis lines=middle, xlabel={$x$}, ylabel={$y$}, xmin=-6, xmax=8, ymin=-8, ymax=6, samples=100, clip=false, axis line style={-{Stealth}}, xtick={-3,3,5}, ytick={-6,-3,3}]
\addplot[smooth, thick, blue, <->, domain=-3.87:5.87] {0.4*(x+3)*(x-5)};
\addplot[dashed, domain=-8:6] (1,x);
\node[above right] at (axis cs:1,3) {$x=1$};
\fill (axis cs:-3,0) circle (2.5pt);
\node[above left] at (axis cs:-3,0) {$-3$};
\end{axis}
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
